1
00:00:19,106 --> 00:00:23,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the topic that Nāna Deepa brought up yesterday was death.

2
00:00:26,251 --> 00:00:32,076
[SPEAKER_03]: And so I thought, what is the problem with death?

3
00:00:32,136 --> 00:00:33,056
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the issue there?

4
00:00:39,681 --> 00:00:40,422
[SPEAKER_06]: What is the issue?

5
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:44,184
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not wanted.

6
00:00:47,207 --> 00:00:48,688
[SPEAKER_05]: At least, what is perceived as.

7
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:57,223
[SPEAKER_06]: So, why would it not be wanted by default?

8
00:01:02,265 --> 00:01:12,489
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's seen as losing the possibility of engaging with things in a pleasurable way.

9
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:17,131
[SPEAKER_06]: And why would that be important?

10
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:21,609
[SPEAKER_06]: Because pleasure is valid.

11
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:32,037
[SPEAKER_06]: So, death is then not wanted and unpleasant only because it interrupts the things that we want.

12
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:35,320
[SPEAKER_03]: More or less.

13
00:01:37,702 --> 00:01:39,924
[SPEAKER_06]: So, what would happen if there's nothing that you want?

14
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:43,987
[SPEAKER_06]: Would death still be a problem?

15
00:01:46,969 --> 00:01:47,190
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

16
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:49,160
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't know.

17
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:59,204
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's the whole... what we spoke about, the upādī and possessions, eh... and goes as deep as your sense organs, your body.

18
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:17,131
[SPEAKER_06]: Not being developed in regard to the body, means taking it up with yours, means depending on it, means wanting what it wants, means basically being within the sixth sense space, within sensuality, eh... which means death.

19
00:02:17,552 --> 00:02:18,352
[SPEAKER_06]: It's eh...

20
00:02:19,785 --> 00:02:24,327
[SPEAKER_06]: It's going to be a problem, because these things are subjected to that cessation, interruption and ending.

21
00:02:25,607 --> 00:02:32,870
[SPEAKER_06]: So then, it's only your ownership that makes you be affected by that problem.

22
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:47,355
[SPEAKER_06]: If you disown, if you abandon your eyes, ear, nose, other senses, if the mind is fully withdrawn from centrality, sees the danger in it, then nothing that death affects can be relevant to you.

23
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:49,673
[SPEAKER_06]: It can cause you pain.

24
00:02:49,974 --> 00:02:51,094
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's the dispassion.

25
00:02:51,354 --> 00:02:52,855
[SPEAKER_06]: Dispassion is the result of practice.

26
00:02:54,496 --> 00:02:59,678
[SPEAKER_06]: Dispassion towards not getting rid of things, not like managing them, whatever else.

27
00:02:59,758 --> 00:02:59,979
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

28
00:02:59,999 --> 00:03:03,961
[SPEAKER_06]: Just being dispassionate towards their arising or ceasing.

29
00:03:04,421 --> 00:03:05,041
[SPEAKER_06]: It doesn't matter.

30
00:03:09,363 --> 00:03:11,124
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how then to develop that dispassion?

31
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:16,867
[SPEAKER_06]: How would one go about it?

32
00:03:19,388 --> 00:03:21,670
[SPEAKER_05]: Sense restraint and virtue.

33
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:24,572
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

34
00:03:24,832 --> 00:03:25,373
[SPEAKER_06]: But what else?

35
00:03:26,414 --> 00:03:32,599
[SPEAKER_06]: So, for most people, when they start, sense restraint and virtue would be just kind of bearing the pain.

36
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:35,541
[SPEAKER_06]: But that's not the enduring we talk about.

37
00:03:36,822 --> 00:03:42,747
[SPEAKER_06]: The endurance we talk about is on that mental level of not acting out, not welcoming, not delighting, as the suttas say.

38
00:03:45,004 --> 00:03:48,105
[SPEAKER_06]: So, bearing needs to come first, yeah?

39
00:03:48,485 --> 00:03:50,445
[SPEAKER_06]: But how do you endure things on the mental level?

40
00:03:51,766 --> 00:03:57,227
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it's not just kind of mindless bearing, like asceticism.

41
00:03:57,447 --> 00:03:58,547
[SPEAKER_06]: How can that become more?

42
00:04:00,208 --> 00:04:01,748
[SPEAKER_06]: By developing understanding.

43
00:04:02,348 --> 00:04:05,109
[SPEAKER_06]: Sure, but of what specifically?

44
00:04:05,169 --> 00:04:06,449
[SPEAKER_06]: Like, how would I do that?

45
00:04:06,489 --> 00:04:09,410
[SPEAKER_06]: So, I am bearing, I'm keeping the precepts, I'm bearing the elements.

46
00:04:10,527 --> 00:04:19,293
[SPEAKER_06]: But, you know, we talk about enduring things on the right level all the time, not trying to get rid of them, not justifying them, not managing them, not psychologizing them.

47
00:04:19,313 --> 00:04:21,614
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how do I learn to endure things on that level?

48
00:04:22,395 --> 00:04:24,216
[SPEAKER_06]: What, what, what can I look for?

49
00:04:24,336 --> 00:04:25,316
[SPEAKER_06]: Where do I look for that?

50
00:04:25,857 --> 00:04:27,718
[SPEAKER_06]: Because all I see is just bearing of the pain.

51
00:04:28,659 --> 00:04:29,659
[SPEAKER_06]: What do I do within that?

52
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,523
[SPEAKER_06]: If I'm not giving in to the management, is there anything I can do?

53
00:04:35,843 --> 00:04:36,964
[SPEAKER_06]: There wouldn't be the management.

54
00:04:37,564 --> 00:04:38,465
[SPEAKER_06]: How do I go about it?

55
00:04:45,062 --> 00:04:46,563
[SPEAKER_05]: Seeing why these things are painful.

56
00:04:47,303 --> 00:04:47,543
[SPEAKER_05]: Sorry?

57
00:04:47,803 --> 00:04:51,365
[SPEAKER_05]: Seeing why these things are painful.

58
00:04:51,965 --> 00:04:53,326
[SPEAKER_05]: Why am I pressured by these things?

59
00:04:53,646 --> 00:04:57,088
[SPEAKER_06]: But that can also fall on the level of management and explanation.

60
00:04:58,649 --> 00:05:01,650
[SPEAKER_06]: So, is that what it would determine?

61
00:05:01,790 --> 00:05:06,893
[SPEAKER_06]: Whether I'm trying to manage my pain, or whether I'm enduring it on the right level, so I see its nature?

62
00:05:08,133 --> 00:05:09,914
[SPEAKER_06]: What determines the management?

63
00:05:10,814 --> 00:05:13,136
[SPEAKER_06]: Is it what I do, or how I do it?

64
00:05:15,046 --> 00:05:18,871
[SPEAKER_06]: Or why I do it.

65
00:05:19,171 --> 00:05:23,977
[SPEAKER_06]: So that's how you look for whether you're trying to get rid of things out of your will, basically.

66
00:05:25,499 --> 00:05:46,294
[SPEAKER_06]: Because whenever there is the urge and pressure to manage the uncomfortable mental states that arise on the bearing of the elements, bearing of the words of others, bearing of the precepts, bearing of the sense restraint, whenever you try to manage means you implicitly want to get rid of it, which means implicitly there is aversion.

67
00:05:47,355 --> 00:05:49,016
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's where you end up revolving around it.

68
00:05:50,137 --> 00:05:55,840
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's why I keep saying like it's about not welcoming, which doesn't mean preventing you from coming at my doorstep.

69
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:57,620
[SPEAKER_06]: That's not within my domain.

70
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:01,042
[SPEAKER_06]: My domain is to not welcome you and not delight in your presence.

71
00:06:01,842 --> 00:06:03,683
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how do I not delight in your presence?

72
00:06:03,743 --> 00:06:06,304
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, I practice the non-delight while you are present.

73
00:06:07,325 --> 00:06:16,089
[SPEAKER_06]: But if I'm concerned about getting rid of you because, you know, I blame you for this disrupted mind, means I am putting the problem where the problem isn't.

74
00:06:18,110 --> 00:06:18,690
[SPEAKER_06]: And, eh...

75
00:06:20,484 --> 00:06:31,909
[SPEAKER_06]: The way to stop doing that is, as we often say, to start actually practicing mindfulness on top of the, you know, sense restraint, enduring the virtue, bearing the virtue precepts and so on.

76
00:06:32,589 --> 00:06:39,812
[SPEAKER_06]: Mindfulness and self-questioning on the level of why am I doing what I'm doing, including trying to manage my dukkha.

77
00:06:41,372 --> 00:06:42,733
[SPEAKER_06]: So, why am I doing it?

78
00:06:43,761 --> 00:06:45,202
[SPEAKER_06]: Is it because I just want to get rid of this pain?

79
00:06:45,342 --> 00:06:52,427
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how is that different from craving against discomfort, aversion towards pain that just keeps fueling everything else?

80
00:06:53,627 --> 00:06:55,148
[SPEAKER_06]: So, that's that right endurance.

81
00:06:56,149 --> 00:06:57,810
[SPEAKER_06]: Like, you recognize it on the level...

82
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:01,632
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it's not determined by what you do, it's determined by why you're doing it.

83
00:07:02,493 --> 00:07:08,517
[SPEAKER_06]: Because, see, somebody can say, yes, I listen to these discussions and I question myself, but why?

84
00:07:08,757 --> 00:07:10,738
[SPEAKER_06]: Oh, because I want to get rid of the pain right here, right now.

85
00:07:11,539 --> 00:07:13,300
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't want to endure it on that mental level.

86
00:07:14,811 --> 00:07:18,442
[SPEAKER_06]: And that means also you're using this as just a way of managing your pain.

87
00:07:18,984 --> 00:07:20,809
[SPEAKER_06]: Of course, everybody will start like that.

88
00:07:21,872 --> 00:07:32,182
[SPEAKER_06]: But as long as you know that that's not a practice, they will lead you to freedom from suffering, it will only result in managing the pain, then you can actually focus on upgrading it.

89
00:07:32,322 --> 00:07:39,049
[SPEAKER_06]: Then you will know that there is an upgrade, which is not acting out of the mental state, but instead being able to endure it.

90
00:07:39,489 --> 00:07:45,295
[SPEAKER_06]: And when the Buddhists talked about that, we mentioned it yesterday, you know, the sutta about the master of the thoughts,

91
00:07:46,418 --> 00:07:49,099
[SPEAKER_06]: And the last one is that, you know, you crush your mind with your mind.

92
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:53,662
[SPEAKER_06]: And people often take that, ah, I just get, get, look, repressed, whatever it is.

93
00:07:53,702 --> 00:07:53,822
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

94
00:07:54,222 --> 00:07:56,363
[SPEAKER_06]: You crush your mind with your mind.

95
00:07:56,423 --> 00:08:01,666
[SPEAKER_06]: In other words, you don't go and get rid of the thoughts that your mind has.

96
00:08:02,967 --> 00:08:07,389
[SPEAKER_06]: And mind, as a sense, does not appear on the level of your attention in the thought.

97
00:08:07,409 --> 00:08:08,450
[SPEAKER_06]: It's always the background.

98
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:10,751
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you crush the background with the background.

99
00:08:11,291 --> 00:08:12,492
[SPEAKER_06]: That's what that statement says.

100
00:08:13,277 --> 00:08:17,740
[SPEAKER_06]: But when people read the mind, they just assume, ah, it's this thought of my mind that I have, that's what I crush.

101
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:19,442
[SPEAKER_06]: So they end up just like, no, no, no.

102
00:08:19,842 --> 00:08:21,683
[SPEAKER_06]: No, actually, it requires wisdom to see that.

103
00:08:22,244 --> 00:08:26,327
[SPEAKER_06]: To endure the mind rightly on the level of the background, you need to see the signs of the mind first.

104
00:08:27,447 --> 00:08:29,989
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's not something you can attend directly.

105
00:08:30,089 --> 00:08:32,451
[SPEAKER_06]: That's something you pick as the background.

106
00:08:33,031 --> 00:08:35,433
[SPEAKER_06]: You know, the simile of the king eating the food.

107
00:08:35,974 --> 00:08:38,475
[SPEAKER_06]: You see the king, you see the food, you don't see anything else.

108
00:08:39,136 --> 00:08:40,036
[SPEAKER_06]: But you notice

109
00:08:40,918 --> 00:08:45,899
[SPEAKER_06]: That he seems to linger along certain dishes more.

110
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:47,880
[SPEAKER_06]: He dismisses others.

111
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:53,942
[SPEAKER_06]: On account of that, you see the background of that behavior is his preference.

112
00:08:54,922 --> 00:08:57,223
[SPEAKER_06]: Oh, he leans towards this, if this is in front.

113
00:08:57,703 --> 00:08:59,583
[SPEAKER_06]: He leans towards that, if this is in front.

114
00:09:00,303 --> 00:09:01,524
[SPEAKER_06]: But you don't see that leaning.

115
00:09:01,624 --> 00:09:02,184
[SPEAKER_06]: You infer it.

116
00:09:02,244 --> 00:09:05,585
[SPEAKER_06]: And you cannot measure it or objectify it.

117
00:09:05,985 --> 00:09:07,565
[SPEAKER_06]: Because it's not... it's not visible.

118
00:09:07,785 --> 00:09:09,546
[SPEAKER_06]: But you can understand it, if you're skilled.

119
00:09:10,612 --> 00:09:16,317
[SPEAKER_06]: The skilled cook would infer the preferences, the signs and tokens of his behavior.

120
00:09:17,718 --> 00:09:18,619
[SPEAKER_06]: Unskilled cook wouldn't.

121
00:09:18,759 --> 00:09:20,180
[SPEAKER_06]: He would just see what's in front of him.

122
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,403
[SPEAKER_06]: Touch, move, eat, do, repeat.

123
00:09:23,483 --> 00:09:24,383
[SPEAKER_06]: He doesn't see anything else.

124
00:09:24,503 --> 00:09:28,767
[SPEAKER_06]: And that's why there is no mind development for such monk, as the Buddha said in that sutta.

125
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:44,289
[SPEAKER_06]: So, it comes down to knowing what you're doing, like, why you're doing what you're doing, and constantly questioning that, so that it will kind of pull you out of that need, pressure, habit to manage dukkha, because that is the natural response, craving against discomfort.

126
00:09:45,310 --> 00:09:48,892
[SPEAKER_06]: So, you need to actually refrain from acting out of that, because it is ill-will.

127
00:09:49,132 --> 00:09:50,333
[SPEAKER_06]: Fundamentally, it is ill-will.

128
00:09:51,374 --> 00:09:59,559
[SPEAKER_06]: You're trying to get rid of the mental states on the level of not like, oh, I tried to get rid of them in a sense, eventually I'm hoping to become an arahant.

129
00:10:00,078 --> 00:10:04,381
[SPEAKER_06]: No, I'm trying to get rid of it, means I'm trying to antidote whatever's currently bothering me.

130
00:10:06,002 --> 00:10:10,946
[SPEAKER_06]: That's basically just action-reaction, just responding with ill-will towards what's unpleasant.

131
00:10:13,447 --> 00:10:20,032
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindfulness of death is... one way of defining that could be to see the sign of death.

132
00:10:20,672 --> 00:10:21,032
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

133
00:10:21,333 --> 00:10:29,921
[SPEAKER_06]: So, even when people do the practice of mindfulness of death, it becomes like this sort of contrived... again, antidoting, responding, managing, doing this.

134
00:10:30,021 --> 00:10:31,102
[SPEAKER_06]: If it's left, I move right.

135
00:10:31,142 --> 00:10:31,982
[SPEAKER_06]: If it's right, I move left.

136
00:10:32,022 --> 00:10:32,163
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

137
00:10:32,503 --> 00:10:38,548
[SPEAKER_06]: It's recognizing the context and then... See, what is the... what are the signs and tokens of your liability to death?

138
00:10:39,589 --> 00:10:40,850
[SPEAKER_06]: Where is the danger there?

139
00:10:41,371 --> 00:10:43,253
[SPEAKER_06]: You realize you can't even conceive

140
00:10:44,877 --> 00:10:47,138
[SPEAKER_06]: How it would be if there were no death.

141
00:10:47,338 --> 00:10:48,678
[SPEAKER_06]: It's just... you can't even imagine that.

142
00:10:48,758 --> 00:10:51,940
[SPEAKER_06]: Yet, here you are, acting as if death won't come your way.

143
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:52,880
[SPEAKER_06]: Why is that?

144
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:58,502
[SPEAKER_06]: That's not going to be measured by your eyes and nose and so on, and your sensations.

145
00:10:58,842 --> 00:10:59,202
[SPEAKER_06]: Exactly.

146
00:10:59,222 --> 00:11:00,363
[SPEAKER_06]: It's a part of the context.

147
00:11:00,563 --> 00:11:03,264
[SPEAKER_06]: So, as we said before, it's that ownership.

148
00:11:04,144 --> 00:11:06,245
[SPEAKER_06]: Oh, it's because things I own.

149
00:11:07,787 --> 00:11:08,787
[SPEAKER_06]: Ownership I assumed.

150
00:11:09,107 --> 00:11:13,829
[SPEAKER_06]: Things I've taken, for the sake of pleasure always, for the sake of safety, comfort, all of that.

151
00:11:14,629 --> 00:11:16,009
[SPEAKER_06]: That's why death is a problem.

152
00:11:16,409 --> 00:11:17,369
[SPEAKER_06]: That's why I fear it.

153
00:11:17,470 --> 00:11:18,230
[SPEAKER_06]: That's why I avoid it.

154
00:11:18,250 --> 00:11:19,690
[SPEAKER_06]: That's why I turn a blind eye to it.

155
00:11:21,090 --> 00:11:25,972
[SPEAKER_06]: So, what if I relinquish my acquisitions, as the suttas would say?

156
00:11:26,792 --> 00:11:29,313
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, oh, death is not a problem.

157
00:11:29,593 --> 00:11:30,913
[SPEAKER_06]: I have gone beyond death.

158
00:11:32,753 --> 00:11:36,055
[SPEAKER_06]: That which is subjected to peril and destruction, these five aggregates, that's it.

159
00:11:36,075 --> 00:11:37,216
[SPEAKER_06]: That's not my problem anymore.

160
00:11:37,616 --> 00:11:39,778
[SPEAKER_06]: When that breaks apart, that's parinibbāna.

161
00:11:44,341 --> 00:11:49,705
[SPEAKER_06]: So, in other words, you don't need to... and this is true for most... all of the contemplations, in a way.

162
00:11:50,085 --> 00:11:52,527
[SPEAKER_06]: You don't need to do the contemplation of death.

163
00:11:52,807 --> 00:11:56,049
[SPEAKER_06]: You just have to discern what's already there that's liable to it.

164
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:59,692
[SPEAKER_06]: And, eh... Why are you not giving it up?

165
00:12:01,551 --> 00:12:02,171
[SPEAKER_06]: As simple as that.

166
00:12:02,351 --> 00:12:05,613
[SPEAKER_06]: Anicca goes down to eye, ear, nose, senses.

167
00:12:06,993 --> 00:12:11,595
[SPEAKER_06]: It's not just a question of, you know, your material objects of the senses that you possess.

168
00:12:11,916 --> 00:12:12,976
[SPEAKER_06]: It's the whole domain.

169
00:12:14,377 --> 00:12:15,497
[SPEAKER_06]: And how can you give up your eyes?

170
00:12:15,537 --> 00:12:16,638
[SPEAKER_06]: Can you just pull them out?

171
00:12:16,738 --> 00:12:18,559
[SPEAKER_06]: In the same sense you would throw your favorite thing?

172
00:12:18,619 --> 00:12:18,799
[SPEAKER_06]: No.

173
00:12:19,419 --> 00:12:24,801
[SPEAKER_06]: So, that's that recognizing of the background and that, well, discernment.

174
00:12:25,201 --> 00:12:28,703
[SPEAKER_06]: Discernment of ownership of that necessary basis for your experience.

175
00:12:29,003 --> 00:12:30,684
[SPEAKER_06]: So, developing this passion towards your body.

176
00:12:41,380 --> 00:13:04,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I ask a question about... because on that, you know, you think in the beginning, like, I want to be free from suffering, I don't want to die, I want to be not liable to death anymore, so I want to go beyond death, but I want to be also free from suffering.

177
00:13:04,271 --> 00:13:08,994
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the sort of... almost... that's the difficulty or the problem, as in you can't...

178
00:13:10,077 --> 00:13:17,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Be free from suffering, or be free from death, without basically relinquishing yourself.

179
00:13:19,059 --> 00:13:24,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why... which is what death is, in a way, the fact that, like, I won't be.

180
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:37,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's hard to practice towards that, while also... because, I mean, in a way, nibbāna, or the idea

181
00:13:38,472 --> 00:13:41,855
[SPEAKER_01]: From the beginning of nibbāna, it's like, my self will not be.

182
00:13:42,315 --> 00:13:45,277
[SPEAKER_01]: That's almost what death is for you.

183
00:13:45,297 --> 00:13:48,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's almost like, kind of, you're aiming towards what you're afraid of.

184
00:13:51,021 --> 00:13:59,147
[SPEAKER_06]: So, how do you... Well, that's the... that's like the... craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving, conceit by the means of conceit.

185
00:13:59,227 --> 00:14:02,750
[SPEAKER_06]: So, yes, you have to want it first, which means it's going to be rooted in craving.

186
00:14:03,711 --> 00:14:07,954
[SPEAKER_06]: Either craving for pleasure of liberation or craving out of the aversion towards pain.

187
00:14:09,227 --> 00:14:11,831
[SPEAKER_06]: So, that's what directs you towards learning the dhamma.

188
